= Hypothesis — Technical =

== Hypothetico-deductive structure ==
A simplified test can be represented as:

<pre>
H ∧ A → P
</pre>

where:
* <code>H</code> is the focal hypothesis;
* <code>A</code> is a conjunction of auxiliary/background assumptions;
* <code>P</code> is a predicted observation.

If <code>P</code> is not observed, classical logic implies that the conjunction <code>H ∧ A</code> is false, not necessarily that <code>H</code> alone is false.

This captures the Duhem–Quine-style problem that tests confront networks of assumptions, measurement practices, and models.

== Falsifiability ==
Popper emphasized risky predictions and falsifiability: a scientific claim should exclude some possible observations rather than be compatible with every outcome.

This is an important norm against ad hoc immunization, but actual scientific inference also uses:
* probabilistic evidence;
* robustness;
* independent replication;
* model comparison;
* parameter estimation;
* Bayesian updating;
* explanatory coherence.

Falsifiability is therefore a useful criterion, not a complete algorithm for science.

== Scientific and statistical hypotheses ==
A scientific hypothesis can concern a mechanism, process, causal relation, classification, or model.

A statistical hypothesis specifies a probability distribution, parameter value/set, or statistical model. In frequentist testing, one may define a null hypothesis <code>H0</code> and alternative <code>H1</code>, then study the behavior of a test statistic under specified assumptions.

Rejecting a statistical null does not by itself establish a scientific causal hypothesis. Study design, measurement validity, model adequacy, multiplicity, and alternative explanations remain relevant.

== Model comparison ==
Hypotheses may be embedded in competing models rather than tested one at a time.

Comparison can use likelihood, predictive performance, Bayes factors/posterior probabilities, information criteria, cross-validation, mechanistic adequacy, or domain-specific evidence. Each method carries different assumptions and should not be treated as a universal measure of truth.

== Causal hypotheses ==
For a causal hypothesis, evidence must support more than association. Experimental intervention, quasi-experimental design, causal identification assumptions, mechanistic evidence, temporal order, and robustness may all matter.

See [[Causality/Technical|Causality — Technical]].

